# ADR-022: Allocator as Sole Gatekeeper — No LBA→Owner Index

**Status**: Accepted (2026-09-07)

## Context

Raised directly: "LBA X to Y is a file — who says that, and what stops I/O from accidentally writing into the middle of it?" The first proposed answer was a dedicated reverse LBA-to-owner lookup structure. Re-examined: every write already has to obtain a free block from the allocator before it can happen at all; the allocator's own free/used tracking is what prevents two writers from ever being handed the same block while it's in use. A reverse-lookup table would only ever be answering a question the write path is already structurally prevented from needing to ask.

## Decision

There is no LBA-to-owner reverse-lookup structure anywhere in the design. Write-collision protection comes entirely from the allocator refusing to hand out any block its free/used tracking (Zone Table, ADR-021, plus finer per-block tracking within a zone) marks as in use. The one place a "does this specific LBA's content already exist elsewhere" question genuinely arises — the LBA-level dedup fallback (ADR-014, "CoW Extreme") — is already answered by the hash-keyed dedup mechanism itself, checked by content hash, never by LBA.

## Rationale

- Directly corrects a proposal made earlier in this same session that solved a problem this design doesn't have.
- Every filesystem's allocator already needs free/used tracking regardless of any indexing decision — this ADR adds no new structure, it removes one that would have been redundant with what the allocator was always going to need anyway.
- Avoids the storage cost a genuine reverse index would have carried — one entry per block on a large drive, versus the Zone Table's one entry per zone.

## Consequences

- **Positive**: removes an entire structure and its consistency-maintenance burden before it was ever built.
- **Negative**: correctness here depends entirely on the allocator's free/used tracking being right, with no independent second structure cross-checking it — mitigated by `fsck`-level consistency checking, not by a standing index.
